{"title":"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A given tetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is isosceles, that is, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/c/57c9800e05234d5c0b9e52ebeaaed47dbc867b43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=CD, AC=BD, AD=BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"263\" height=\"16\" />. Show that the faces of the tetrahedron are acute-angled triangles.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/5/f75d6d58b03031bd7cb52a7ca886a8ddf369d21c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" /> is fixed.\nBy the equality conditions, it follows that the maximal possible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> occurs when the four vertices are coplanar, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> on the opposite side of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/b/cfbb4c52e011ceed6e813118a7d59e931af444a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AD}$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />.\nIn this case, the tetrahedron is not actually a tetrahedron, so this maximum isn't actually attainable.\n</p><p>For the sake of contradiction, suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93e72b9d2c86f197276a931aba77c472d0511c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABD$\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" /> is non-acute.\nThen, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/d/84dbfeb0352111a94f99e56c2f76e185d2020f96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(AD)^2\\geq (AB)^2+(BD)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"201\" height=\"21\" />.\nIn our optimal case noted above, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/f/b5fa2b4aca7257e2460e27a4910f42731cbc3fe1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACDB$\" width=\"57\" height=\"13\" /> is a parallelogram, so\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/a/62a590b3d422a8eef787de60c2b81bb1f29594d4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} 2(BD)^2 + 2(AB)^2 &amp;= (AD)^2 + (CB)^2 \\\\ &amp;= 2(AD)^2 \\\\ &amp;\\geq 2(BD)^2+2(AB)^2.  \\end{align*}\" width=\"311\" height=\"75\" />\nHowever, as stated, equality cannot be attained, so we get our desired contradiction.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>It's not hard to see that the four faces are congruent from SSS Congruence. Without loss of generality, assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/b/0cb85f4c65b2e3421d46d58ac8816b1935c8890a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB\\leq BC \\leq CA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"133\" height=\"15\" />. Now assume, for the sake of contradiction, that each face is non-acute; that is, right or obtuse. Consider triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/5/f75d6d58b03031bd7cb52a7ca886a8ddf369d21c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" />. They share side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/2/9b25f8e64b484493fda944d25cad453423041fe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l$\" width=\"5\" height=\"12\" /> be the planes passing through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, respectively, that are perpendicular to side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. We have that triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> are non-acute, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are not strictly between planes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/2/9b25f8e64b484493fda944d25cad453423041fe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l$\" width=\"5\" height=\"12\" />. Therefore the length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is at least the distance between the planes, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. However, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/e/0ae74715a618ba7e72d29c5a553325ebcea05c68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD=AB$\" width=\"81\" height=\"13\" />, then the four points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are coplanar, and the volume of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> would be zero. Therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/2/4b23208110279c88dc8fcd52acef41184cbf2288.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD&gt;AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"81\" height=\"13\" />. However, we were given that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/e/0ae74715a618ba7e72d29c5a553325ebcea05c68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD=AB$\" width=\"81\" height=\"13\" /> in the problem, which leads to a contradiction. Therefore the faces of the tetrahedron must all be acute.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/a/a9a11161df4592d314bda7e9ef31be7796ae50b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{a} = \\overrightarrow{DA}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"21\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/5/745b66bc1311d0271e68d3bc09e8616a1ac23ad1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{b} = \\overrightarrow{DB}$\" width=\"61\" height=\"22\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/1/0e126efa151a866963f19d7208e299937abbcbff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{c} = \\overrightarrow{DC}$\" width=\"61\" height=\"22\" />. The conditions given translate to\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/c/0cccfa6155a71fa9dd60f7d79cf1e826b4c8cf50.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{a} &amp;= \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{b} + \\vec{c}\\cdot\\vec{c} - 2(\\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{c}) \\\\ \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{b} &amp;= \\vec{c}\\cdot\\vec{c} + \\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{a} - 2(\\vec{c}\\cdot\\vec{a}) \\\\ \\vec{c}\\cdot\\vec{c} &amp;= \\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{a} + \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{b} - 2(\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{b}) \\end{align*}\" width=\"214\" height=\"83\" />\nWe wish to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/a/35abd4b24aceb4ea9dbc9d96a1f2a177a6d0066e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{b}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/e/89e018189f855042dae3c15dea47a269b2f70435.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{c}$\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e4551e5afd4f8aa338be3b6b67a58a9038c8cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{c}\\cdot\\vec{a}$\" width=\"31\" height=\"13\" /> are all positive. WLOG, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/4/2247fa013721d987b19bb28eb59c9a50f46d9213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{a}\\geq \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{b}, \\vec{c}\\cdot\\vec{c} &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"155\" height=\"21\" />, so it immediately follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/a/35abd4b24aceb4ea9dbc9d96a1f2a177a6d0066e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{b}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/73997b5e23956bbcbe77ed482dcbce4002d7d08a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{c}$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> are positive. Adding all three equations,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/9/2893232196e53a02a06a92a63491ec09868f684b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{a} + \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{b} + \\vec{c}\\cdot\\vec{c} = 2(\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{b} + \\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{c} + \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{c})\\]\" width=\"314\" height=\"22\" />\nIn addition,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/b/43be80ae90ab735c441bcfd15f795017fa152286.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} (\\vec{a} - \\vec{b} - \\vec{c})\\cdot(\\vec{a} - \\vec{b} - \\vec{c})&amp;\\geq 0 \\\\ \\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{a} + \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{b} + \\vec{c}\\cdot\\vec{c}&amp;\\geq 2(\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{b} + \\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{c} - \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{c}) \\\\ 2(\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{b} + \\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{c} + \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{c})&amp;\\geq 2(\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{b} + \\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{c} - \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{c}) \\\\ \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{c}&amp;\\geq 0 \\end{align*}\" width=\"359\" height=\"111\" />\nEquality could only occur if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/9/f29a3e08e5444842a5c9c486aa7aede73f7c666d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{a} = \\vec{b} + \\vec{c}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"74\" height=\"19\" />, which requires the vectors to be coplanar and the original tetrahedron to be degenerate.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose for the sake of contradiction that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/6/5862947b38a495ec9be80e5b9c7582de6cf0d269.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> is not acute. Since all three sides of triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/d/2edcdc0d73ef64ec441f60a3a8dccdbd10aa61a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BAC$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/4/fd43b1261ea6db8d43a9bdbd28f90cf6f23617d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CDB$\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> are congruent, those two triangles are congruent, meaning <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/1/e21c625c501dd5d7f3af600cccc76c7137f70f53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BDC=\\angle BAC&gt;90^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"185\" height=\"13\" />. Construct a sphere with diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />. Since angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/d/2edcdc0d73ef64ec441f60a3a8dccdbd10aa61a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BAC$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/5/4057c3e63298c2ed6715003afb6f2d9dc6272037.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BDC$\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> are both not acute, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> both lie on or inside the sphere. We seek to make <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/4/314bfa1fb251cfe31dbff4a462217c7e4ebce5b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD=BC$\" width=\"82\" height=\"13\" /> to satisfy the conditions of the problem. This can only occur when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is a diameter of the sphere, since both points lie on or inside the sphere. However, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> to be a diameter, all four points must be coplanar, as all diameters intersect at the center of the sphere. This would make tetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> degenerate, creating a contradiction. Thus, all angles on a face of an isosceles tetrahedron are acute.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Proof by contradiction: Assume at least one of the tetrahedron's faces are obtuse. WLOG, assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/6/5862947b38a495ec9be80e5b9c7582de6cf0d269.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> is an obtuse angle. Using SSS congruence to prove that all four faces of the tetrahedron are congruent also shows that the angles surrounding point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> are congruent to angles in triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />; namely, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/5/cb55dc9c50a80e1d9eb23fe53e45f464c0ac755e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" /> is congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3ad57710b6810476e6017ea2c2cc6d41219c218.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/1/4f105551f628f107282e0cc6c89063f3c1faece9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAD$\" width=\"55\" height=\"13\" /> is congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/2/0728bebb477a2636300c56851b6a6f9e382c1b83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" />. Since the internal angles of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> must add to 180 degrees, so do the angles surrounding point A. Now lay triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> on a flat surface. A diagram would make it clear that from the perspective of an aerial view, the \"apparent\" measures of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/5/cb55dc9c50a80e1d9eb23fe53e45f464c0ac755e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/1/4f105551f628f107282e0cc6c89063f3c1faece9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAD$\" width=\"55\" height=\"13\" /> (which are most likely distorted visually, assuming these angles stick up vertically from the flat surface on which triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> lies) can never exceed the true measures of those angles (equality happens when these angles also lie flat on top of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />). This means that these two angles can never join to form side AD (because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/6/5862947b38a495ec9be80e5b9c7582de6cf0d269.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> is more than the sum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/5/cb55dc9c50a80e1d9eb23fe53e45f464c0ac755e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/1/4f105551f628f107282e0cc6c89063f3c1faece9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAD$\" width=\"55\" height=\"13\" /> - a direct consequence of the facts that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/6/5862947b38a495ec9be80e5b9c7582de6cf0d269.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> is obtuse and all three angles add up to 180 degrees), so the tetrahedron with obtuse triangle faces is impossible.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6\">Solution 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Lemma: given triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, which we will call <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />, we can say that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/5/fd5925723767a79a8979d7689cb6d55e5305e961.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM &gt; \\frac{BC}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"92\" height=\"39\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/7/327ed4999262d30b5af5595c450634cc468942f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A &lt; 90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"71\" height=\"15\" />.\nProof: Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bfb551ca0f4c15607ad073fcc0cb9c1d9a61c07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BM = MC = \\frac{BC}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"152\" height=\"39\" />. Since it is given that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/5/fd5925723767a79a8979d7689cb6d55e5305e961.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM &gt; \\frac{BC}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"92\" height=\"39\" />, we can substitute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/1/ec1802271290c7691bd12d5b981b30265075e28a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BC}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"31\" height=\"37\" /> to get two inequalities:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/2/9927fa7347e648a73667788c5eafffe39eb51a43.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AM &gt; CM, \\quad AM &gt; BM.\\]\" width=\"209\" height=\"16\" />\nThe above inequalities imply that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/7/c5792f5d65d2adc9a25413249ec68451adb2d60e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle C &gt; \\angle CAM$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"114\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/e/7fe4c4ec9323896a1c265d2ea3c1496d2f456bf5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle B &gt; \\angle BAM$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"114\" height=\"15\" />. Adding these inequalities and simplifying the RHS, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/c/14c0b9ad58e3416f619eeb5f4fb5426b948af3ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle C + \\angle B &gt; \\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"129\" height=\"16\" />. Adding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b1ac662f7ddbbce489953c8b7693848239564aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"14\" /> to both sides, replacing the LHS with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/b/5cb1b41850ab676e4d1cf15c69b1609af4befbc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> and dividing by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> gets us that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/7/327ed4999262d30b5af5595c450634cc468942f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A &lt; 90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"71\" height=\"15\" />. This is our desired inequality, so we are done.\nNote that all faces of this tetrahedron are congruent, by SSS.\nIn particular, we will use that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/9/3195f5d02075d517e0fc7db84a7ed6563ae938e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD \\cong \\triangle BAC$\" width=\"142\" height=\"13\" />. WLOG, assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/718105456aa41c974333e9ffe73a31053003c68b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ADB$\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" /> is the largest angle in triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />.\nBecause <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/9/3195f5d02075d517e0fc7db84a7ed6563ae938e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD \\cong \\triangle BAC$\" width=\"142\" height=\"13\" />, the median from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is equal length to the median from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. These points meet at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. By the triangle inequality,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/6/156599da087080d41a728816804f2b177634307b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[DE + CE &gt; CD.\\]\" width=\"138\" height=\"14\" />By substituting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> (this is a given in the problem) and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/5/6d5c9afa92ae3180f02754c177e530d9d0f8b4d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />, and then dividing by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we get that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/7/747a86a4a445a414001eb97e538b4caa712cbe36.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[DE &gt; \\frac{AB}{2}.\\]\" width=\"89\" height=\"37\" />By the lemma we showed at the start, this implies that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/a/e7aba14de231889d060663ceee088ea7e936d225.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ADB &lt; 90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"101\" height=\"15\" />, and since we said that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/718105456aa41c974333e9ffe73a31053003c68b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ADB$\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" /> was the largest angle, triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/1/8810a26f59e2c67b5f2ff052a7e2a294fef0a03e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ADB$\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> must be acute. Since all of the faces of this tetrahedron are congruent, then, all of the faces must be acute.\n</p><p><i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO\" title=\"1972 USAMO\">1972 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1972\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261016222341\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.053 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.076 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 652/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1617/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 21053/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   18.836      1 -total\n 36.53%    6.881      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n 35.92%    6.766      1 Template:USAMO_box\n 26.18%    4.932      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:7524:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261016222341 and revision id 172201. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}